course: world
lesson: 177
title: "Crime is up, I saw it on the news"
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The news is a list of the most unusual and frightening things that happened somewhere in the whole country today; the ordinary, safe days of millions of people are not news.
explain: Watch 300 crime stories in a year and your brain starts to feel that crime is everywhere, even if none of them happened on your street. What you see a lot feels common; grown-ups call this the availability trap.
explain: Plane crashes are on the news because they are rare; car crashes are so common they hardly make it. So Uncle Paul drives five hours to feel safe and picks the riskier trip.
explain: To know how common something really is, you need counts, not stories: official figures, the real numbers behind a percentage, and what exactly was counted.
explain: "Burglaries up 50%!" sounds terrifying until you hear it went from 2 to 3, and the third one was Dave's shed, and Dave left the door open.
fact: The news picks unusual, scary stories because they are surprising, and that makes them feel more common than they are.
fact: Official figures show that many kinds of crime have gone down over the years, even while many people feel things are getting worse.
fact: Shark attacks are very rare everywhere and almost unheard of around Britain; strong currents, sunburn and jellyfish are much more common risks at the beach.
fact: For the same distance, flying on a big airline is much safer than going by car.
fact: Most dogs never bite anyone; learning how to be safe around dogs is more useful than fearing all of them.
fact: A scary screen-time number may count schoolwork and a TV left on in the background, so always ask what exactly was counted.
fact: When numbers are small, percentages jump: 2 burglaries becoming 3 is a rise of 50%, but it is only one more burglary.
fact: Far fewer children are killed on Britain's roads today than in the 1970s.
fact: Reporters often pick dramatic people to interview; one angry man on TV doesn't show what most people think, but a fair survey can.
fact: Before keeping everyone home, check the local picture: if only one child in the class is ill, the illness isn't spreading widely there.
fact: People have complained that "young people have no manners" for thousands of years; most teenagers are polite most of the time.
fact: Words people use: "telly" (UK) means TV; "bloke" (UK, Australia) means a man; "proper" (UK) means really; "back in my day" is how older people start talking about their childhood; "highkey" (US, young people) means very much, openly; "yinz" (Pittsburgh) means "you all"; "fr" means "for real".
exercise: Grandma watches the news every night and says, "Crime is everywhere now." What do official figures show? | Many kinds of crime have gone down over the years | Crime has doubled every single year since she was young | Crime only happens in the places shown on TV | Nobody counts crime, so there are no figures
exercise: Why does crime feel everywhere when you watch lots of news? | The news shows the scariest stories from the whole country every night | Crime only happens in the evening when the news is on | TV news only reports crimes from your own street | Newsreaders make up stories to fill the time
exercise: What do grown-ups call it when the things you see a lot start to feel common? | The availability trap | The straw-man argument | The fence-post error in counting | The famous birthday paradox
exercise: After a shark story, Ellie's mum won't let her swim on holiday in Cornwall. What is true? | Shark attacks around Britain are almost unheard of | Sharks bite British swimmers on most summer holidays | Cornwall's sea is the most dangerous in the world | Sharks only bite children who swim on holiday
exercise: Which are more common risks at a British beach than sharks? | Strong currents, sunburn and jellyfish | Crocodiles, whales and giant octopuses | Falling coconuts and polar bears | None; sharks are the most common risk
exercise: Uncle Paul drives five hours because "planes crash all the time, I see it on the news". What is true? | For the same distance, flying on a big airline is far safer | Driving is always safer than flying, especially on long motorway trips | Planes crash every day, so he is quite right | Both are exactly as dangerous for the same distance
exercise: Why are plane crashes on the news so often? | Because they are rare and surprising | Because they happen more often than car crashes | Because cars never crash on motorways | Because pilots ask to be on TV
exercise: Dad reads one headline about a dog bite: "All dogs are dangerous now." What is wrong? | One story is turned into a rule about all dogs | One story is enough to know everything about every dog | Dogs really have become much more dangerous this year | He should have read two stories first, then decided
exercise: "Kids spend 7 HOURS a day on screens!" The article counts schoolwork and TV in the background. What should Tom's mum ask? | What exactly was counted as screen time | Which phone the children were using | Whether the article was printed in colour | How many hours grown-ups sleep
exercise: "Burglaries up 50%!" Last year there were 2 in the village, this year 3. How many more burglaries is that? | One | Fifty | Two | Three
exercise: Why can a percentage look huge in a small village? | With tiny numbers, one extra case is a big percentage | Villages always count every crime twice | Percentages only work properly for big cities | 50% always means fifty more crimes
exercise: Grandpa: "Back in my day, children were safer." What do the road numbers show? | Far fewer children die on Britain's roads now than in the 1970s | Far more children are killed on Britain's roads now than in the 1970s | Exactly as many children die on roads as in the 1970s | Nobody has counted road accidents since the 1970s
exercise: A TV report shows one angry man shouting about a new rule. Ruby's dad: "See, everybody hates it." What would show what most people think? | A fair survey of lots of people | Interviewing the same man again | The loudest person in the car park | Timing how long the man shouted
exercise: Mum keeps the kids home from a party because of flu on the news, but only one child in their class is ill. What is the sensible step? | Check the local picture before keeping everyone home | Never go to any party all winter | Believe the news over what you see at school | Keep everyone home whenever flu is on TV
exercise: Grandad: "Teenagers these days have no manners." His teenage granddaughter brings him tea. What is true? | Most teenagers are polite most of the time | Teenagers have never been polite at all, not even once | Only teenagers who appear on the news are polite | Manners disappeared a few years ago, with phones
exercise: How long have people complained that young people have no manners? | For thousands of years | Only since phones were invented | Only since the 1970s | Only since last year
test: Nan in Yorkshire: "I'm not going out after dark, love, crime's proper everywhere, I see it every night on the telly." What is the fair reply? | The news gathers scary stories from everywhere; counts show how common crime is | She's right; nightly news means crime is on her street | She should watch even more news to be sure | Crime only happens after dark, so she's right
test: Young cousin: "Yo, sharks lowkey everywhere in Cornwall, I saw a vid, I'm not swimming fr." What is the truth? | Shark bites around Britain are almost unheard of | Cornwall has more shark bites every year than Australia | One video proves that sharks are everywhere | Sharks only come close when children swim
test: What would happen if the news showed a story about every safe car trip and every safe flight? | It would never end, which is why news shows only the rare things | Plane crashes would become more common | Nobody would ever dare to fly again | The roads would suddenly get more dangerous
test: Uncle Paul: "Planes? Nah mate, I'll drive, it's safer." Then he drives five hours on the motorway. Which is true? | He chose the riskier trip, just slower | He chose the safest possible way to travel | Driving and flying are equally risky for the same distance | Motorways never have any accidents
test: Aunt: "Burglaries up 50%! We're doomed!" Last year 2, this year 3. What do you say? | It's one more burglary; ask for the real numbers | It means 50 more houses in the village were burgled | It means half the houses in the village were burgled | It means the number of burglaries more than doubled
test: Grandpa from Pittsburgh: "Back in my day yinz kids played out all day, way safer." What is the fair answer? | Some things were better then, but roads are much safer for children now | Everything was safer in the 1970s | Nothing at all was better in the past | Children never played outside back then
test: Ruby: "Dad, it's one bloke in a car park." Dad: "See, everybody hates the rule." Who is reasoning better? | Ruby; one person on TV doesn't show what everybody thinks | Dad; reporters always pick a typical person who speaks for all | Dad; if one man is angry, everyone must be | Neither; you can never find out what people think
test: In a cartoon Grandad sees two news stories, and his thought bubble shows every teenager in the world throwing eggs. Put the cartoon right. | Two stories about a few people don't describe millions | Two stories are enough to describe every teenager in the world | Only teenagers in cartoons have no manners | Grandad must have seen every teenager on TV
test: Mum: "Flu's highkey everywhere, no party, end of." Only one kid in the class is ill. What is fair? | Look at the local picture; one ill child is not an outbreak | Keep everyone at home for the whole winter | Go to the party even if you are ill yourself | Flu on TV means everyone you meet this week is already ill too
test: "Seven hours on screens a day?! Tom, you're glued to that thing." The number includes the whiteboard at school. What should Mum do? | Read past the headline to see what was counted | Ban the whiteboard at school so the number goes down | Believe the headline number exactly as it is printed | Count only the hours Tom spends on his own phone
test: Dad: "One dog bit someone, it's in the paper. No dog for us, ever." The dog next door is a 14-year-old sausage dog. What is the better plan? | Learn how to be safe around dogs instead of fearing them all | Never walk past any dog again | Make every dog wear a muzzle at home | Believe every headline about animals
exam: Identify the principal reason for which persons who watch a great deal of news frequently overestimate the prevalence of crime. | The news selects unusual and alarming events from a very large area | Crime occurs predominantly at the hour at which the news is broadcast | News broadcasts report only the crimes in the viewer's street | Official crime figures are not compiled by any authority
exam: It is given that burglaries in a village increased from two in one year to three in the next. Determine the number of additional burglaries thereby represented. | One additional burglary | Fifty additional burglaries | Two additional burglaries | Three additional burglaries
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the comparative safety, over the same distance travelled, of air travel on major airlines and travel by car. | Air travel on major airlines is considerably safer | Travel by car is considerably safer | Both are precisely equal in safety | No comparison has ever been made
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the inference that a new rule is universally disliked because one angry man was shown on television? | One interviewee does not establish the opinion of the population | A single interviewee is sufficient to establish the opinion of the public | Reporters invariably select the most typical citizen | Public opinion cannot be measured by any method
